# Copy to .env.local (gitignored). Never commit real values.

# --- Model provider (Azure OpenAI) — required for the agent to answer ---
AZURE_AI_CHATBOT_OPENAI_ENDPOINT=
AZURE_AI_CHATBOT_API_KEY=
AZURE_AI_CHATBOT_DEPLOYMENT_NAME=gpt-4o-mini

# --- AI Gateway routing (MVP plan, Layer 5: the hard spend cap) ---
# Set to a Vercel AI Gateway model id to route model calls through the gateway,
# which activates the hard spend cap + per-visitor cost tracking. Keep Azure EU
# as the provider behind it via BYOK (configured in the Gateway dashboard).
# Leave BLANK to call the Azure deployment directly (default; no spend cap).
#   e.g. AI_GATEWAY_MODEL=openai/gpt-4o-mini
AI_GATEWAY_MODEL=
# Off Vercel only: a static gateway key (on Vercel, OIDC is automatic — leave blank).
# AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=

# --- Prompt caching (informational — there is NO duration knob to set) ---
# The ~16.7k-token system prompt is cached AUTOMATICALLY by Azure OpenAI
# (gpt-4o-mini, api-version >= 2024-10-01): a warm request reuses the prefix at ~75%
# lower cost. It is PROVIDER-MANAGED — Azure keeps it ~5-10 min and refreshes on
# the next request; there is no TTL/duration setting to tune here or in the API.
# Verify anytime with `npm run cache:check`; the hit count appears in each trace
# as `app.tokens.cached`. (Note: LangSmith's dashboard $ over-reports cost — it
# prices raw tokens and ignores the cache discount; trust app.cost.estimate_usd.)
#
# If you want a cache lifetime YOU control, that means a RESPONSE cache (store
# whole answers to repeat questions), NOT prompt caching — via the Vercel AI
# Gateway `cacheControl: max-age=…` or a small app/edge cache. That is where a
# RESPONSE_CACHE_TTL_SEC knob would live; ask to add it if you want it.

# --- Execution budgets (production-readiness review §P2.6) ---
# Per-session token caps enforced by eve (agent/agent.ts). A public anonymous
# endpoint must be bounded: eve's default is 40M input tokens/session (~$80) with
# output UNCAPPED. Support sessions are short, so we cap far lower. Set to 0 to
# disable a cap (uncapped). Blank = use the built-in defaults shown here.
NAVIO_MAX_INPUT_TOKENS_PER_SESSION=250000
NAVIO_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS_PER_SESSION=20000
# Max bytes accepted on any chat request body (agent/channels/eve.ts). Blocks
# oversized-message abuse before the model is called. 0 disables. Default 16000.
NAVIO_MAX_REQUEST_BYTES=16000

# Max CHARACTERS in a single chat message (lib/message-limits.ts). Finer than the
# byte cap above: this is the number the widget's counter shows AND the number the
# API enforces, on both the FAQ channel and the /api/partner proxy — one value so
# the UI can never promise what the server would reject.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ is required: Next inlines it into the browser bundle and exposes it
# to the server, so both sides read the same value. Default 300 (~3-4 sentences,
# the shape of a chat turn). The contact form has its own, larger MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS.
NEXT_PUBLIC_NAVIO_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS=300

# --- Public widget security (MVP plan, Layers 1 & 4) ---
# Extra browser origins allowed to call /eve/v1/* IN ADDITION to this deployment's
# own origin (the iframe is same-origin, so usually leave BLANK). Comma-separated.
# Only needed if you serve the widget cross-origin.
#   e.g. WIDGET_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://www.sportnavi.de,https://sportnavi.de
WIDGET_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=
# Who may EMBED the widget iframe on their site — the allow-list of websites that
# are permitted to display the Navio chat window. Enforced by the browser via the
# `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors` header on /widget. Any site NOT listed
# here CANNOT show the widget (the browser refuses the iframe). Space-separated origins.
#   'self'                                        = the widget's own page (always keep)
#   https://www.sportnavi.de https://sportnavi.de = the live Sportnavi site
# Add more origins to let other sites embed it (e.g. a partner site, or a local test page):
#   WIDGET_FRAME_ANCESTORS='self' https://www.sportnavi.de https://sportnavi.de http://localhost:8080
# Local dev, so a test page (embed-test/simple.html) on any common dev port can embed the widget
# (production keeps only localhost:3001 + :8080):
#   WIDGET_FRAME_ANCESTORS='self' http://localhost:3000 http://localhost:3001 http://localhost:5173 http://localhost:8080 https://www.sportnavi.de https://sportnavi.de
# Leave BLANK to use the built-in default, which is exactly the value below (sportnavi.de only).
WIDGET_FRAME_ANCESTORS='self' https://www.sportnavi.de https://sportnavi.de
# Bot protection (Vercel BotID). Requires the `botid` package + Vercel Pro.
# Server-side enforcement gate in agent/channels/eve.ts:
BOTID_ENABLED=false
# Client-side challenge injection in the widget page (must match the server flag):
NEXT_PUBLIC_BOTID_ENABLED=false
# Public policy links shown in the widget (consent gate, footer, contact-form consent).
# Defaults already point at the live Sportnavi pages; override only if the URLs move.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIVACY_URL=https://www.sportnavi.de/datenschutz/
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WIDERRUF_URL=https://www.sportnavi.de/widerrufsbelehrung/

# --- "Book a Meeting" menu option (Microsoft Bookings) ---
# Public self-service Bookings page opened in a new tab; client-exposed on
# purpose (it's a public booking link, not a secret — no Graph API credentials
# are used here). UNSET = the menu card is hidden, same pattern as
# PARTNER_AGENT_HOST. Today this points at the single "Corporate Demo"
# appointment type; there is no in-widget type picker.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BOOKING_URL=

# --- Partner agent menu option (proxy target) ---
# The "Partner finden" menu option proxies /api/partner/* to the Partner Recommendation
# agent — the CONVEX build at SportnaviPartnerRecomandationBot/partner-recommendation-agent-convex,
# which runs as a SEPARATE service/deploy. Point this at that project's eve host.
# UNSET = the option is effectively disabled (the proxy returns 503) and the KB widget
# still works. NEVER this widget's own port (3001) — that self-proxy makes the FAQ agent
# silently answer partner questions (CortexKit CLAUDE.md §10.6). Use 127.0.0.1, not
# localhost (Node fetch may pick IPv6). Local demo: run the partner agent on its own free
# port (3005 here — 3002 is taken by the sportnavi-web-local Docker container) and set:
PARTNER_AGENT_HOST=http://127.0.0.1:3005

# --- Langfuse (self-hosted) — THE observability backend ---
# Traces go out as plain OTLP to <LANGFUSE_BASE_URL>/api/public/otel/v1/traces with
# Basic auth. Missing host or key = every Langfuse surface stays a silent no-op and
# the agent still runs, so a fresh clone needs none of this.
# Keys: Langfuse UI → your project → Settings → API keys.
LANGFUSE_BASE_URL=https://sportnavi-langfuse.sportnavi.de
LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=
LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=
# Score-config + annotation-queue ids for the 👍/👎 feedback system (optional).
# Get these by running `npx tsx scripts/setup-feedback-scores.ts` — it prints
# them. Unset = feedback still works today, just without the scores linked to
# their declared config and without negative feedback queuing for review.
LANGFUSE_FEEDBACK_SCORE_CONFIG_ID=
LANGFUSE_REASON_SCORE_CONFIG_ID=
LANGFUSE_FEEDBACK_QUEUE_ID=
# Separates dev/preview/production traces in the UI. Defaults to VERCEL_ENV, then
# NODE_ENV. Must match ^(?!langfuse)[a-z0-9-_]+$ and be <=40 chars, or Langfuse
# rejects the span — the code normalizes anything that doesn't.
LANGFUSE_TRACING_ENVIRONMENT=development
# true ships full prompts and completions (i.e. visitor text) to Langfuse.
# Default private: this is a public, anonymous widget.
LANGFUSE_RECORD_IO=false
# Optional dials, all safe to leave unset:
#   LANGFUSE_TRACE_COMPLETENESS=complete  also export eve's workflow/step/fetch spans.
#     Measured cost: one FAQ turn goes from ~10 to 112 observations, 89 of them
#     transport chatter. Only for debugging eve itself.
#   LANGFUSE_DEDUPE_USAGE=false      keep aggregator token usage (inflates cost ~2x)
#   LANGFUSE_HUMAN_NAMES=false       keep raw framework span names
#   LANGFUSE_EXPORT_ALL=true         bypass the span filter when debugging
#   EVE_LF_SPAN_DEBUG=1              log every KEEP/DROP decision to .data/spans.log

# --- LangSmith — EVAL PIPELINE ONLY (no longer used for tracing) ---
# Tracing moved to Langfuse above. These remain solely because the eval scripts
# (eval:upload / eval:verify / eval:run) still store datasets and experiments in
# LangSmith. Phase 2 migrates them to Langfuse datasets + scores, after which this
# block and the `langsmith` dependency go away. Leave blank if you don't run evals.
LANGSMITH_API_KEY=
LANGSMITH_PROJECT=kb-agent-langsmith-starter
LANGSMITH_ENDPOINT=https://eu.api.smith.langchain.com

# --- Contact form (Kontaktformular → Salesforce) — SERVER-ONLY, never NEXT_PUBLIC_ ---
# Leave BOTH Salesforce credentials BLANK for SIMULATE MODE: the form works end-to-end
# and returns success without contacting Salesforce (payload logged at WARNING). Fill
# them (in Vercel env / .env.local, never committed) to create real Cases.
SALESFORCE_CLIENT_ID=
SALESFORCE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Non-secret Salesforce config (change per org):
SALESFORCE_TOKEN_URL=https://sportnavi.my.salesforce.com/services/oauth2/token
SALESFORCE_INSTANCE_URL=https://sportnavi.my.salesforce.com
SALESFORCE_API_VERSION=v65.0
SALESFORCE_FLOW_API_NAME=CaseHandler
SALESFORCE_TOKEN_TTL_SEC=3600
SALESFORCE_TIMEOUT_SEC=15
# Message length cap + per-IP/minute rate limit for POST /api/contact:
MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS=2000
CONTACT_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN=15
# Optional email fallback (used only if Salesforce fails). Not wired yet — leaving blank
# logs the payload at ERROR instead. Add `nodemailer` + these to enable email fallback.
CONTACT_FALLBACK_EMAIL=mohamed-naceur.mahmoud@sportnavi.de
# SMTP_HOST=
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_STARTTLS=true

# --- Monitoring & Alerting (Langfuse Monitors → Teams + Email) ---
# Langfuse's native Monitors/Alerts notify Slack, a generic Webhook, or GitHub Actions —
# no native Teams or email. app/api/monitoring/alerts/{faq,partner,orchestrator}/route.ts
# receives each project's signed webhook and fans it out to Teams + email. See
# docs/MONITORING-ALERTING.md for full setup. Every var below is independently optional
# and independently no-op'd — unset ⇒ that piece is silently skipped, same invariant as
# every other Langfuse surface in this repo.
#
# One secret per Langfuse project (Monitors → Automations → Webhook → the secret shown
# at creation). Unset ⇒ that project's relay route verifies nothing and no-ops (200, does
# not forward anywhere) rather than trusting an unsigned request.
LANGFUSE_ALERT_WEBHOOK_SECRET_FAQ=
LANGFUSE_ALERT_WEBHOOK_SECRET_PARTNER=
LANGFUSE_ALERT_WEBHOOK_SECRET_ORCHESTRATOR=
# Teams incoming webhook URL (channel → "..." → Workflows app → "Webhookbenachrichtigungen
# an einen Kanal senden" template; the relay sends Adaptive Card attachments — see
# docs/MONITORING-ALERTING.md). Unset ⇒ Teams delivery skipped; email (if configured) still fires.
TEAMS_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Microsoft Graph — reuses the existing "navio-chatbot" Azure AD app registration
# (Application permission Mail.Send, admin-consented; see docs/MONITORING-ALERTING.md).
# Prefix matches docs/NAVIO-MULTI-AGENT-ARCHITECTURE.md's MS_GRAPH_* convention, already
# reserved on this same app for a future calendar-booking feature. Unset ⇒ email delivery
# skipped; Teams (if configured) still fires.
MS_GRAPH_TENANT_ID=
MS_GRAPH_CLIENT_ID=
MS_GRAPH_CLIENT_SECRET=
MS_GRAPH_SENDER_UPN=
# Comma-separated recipient list for alert emails (the on-call distribution list).
ALERT_EMAIL_TO=
# Optional cap on the alert-webhook request body. Blank = built-in default (64000).
# MONITORING_MAX_REQUEST_BYTES=
